lg:DE-DLInfoV-s2-1-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "8d370ed17f2f65a0fe9a39e1c6d41ccc200be87def0c5644c46ccbc332d1c413" ;
    lgo:text """(1) Without prejudice to further requirements under other legal provisions, a service provider must make the following information available to a service recipient in a clear and comprehensible form before a written contract is concluded or, if no written contract is concluded, before the service is provided:
1. its surname and first name, and for partnerships with legal capacity and legal persons the company name stating the legal form,
2. the address of its establishment or, if there is no establishment, an address for service of process, and further details enabling the service recipient to contact it quickly and directly, in particular a telephone number and an e-mail address or fax number,
3. if it is entered in one, the commercial register, register of cooperatives, register of civil-law partnerships, partnership register or register of associations, stating the registry court and the registration number,
4. for activities requiring a permit, the name and address of the competent authority or of the point of single contact,
5. if it holds a VAT identification number under § 27a of the Value Added Tax Act, that number,
6. if the service is provided in the exercise of a regulated profession within the meaning of Article 3(1)(a) of Directive 2005/36/EC of the European Parliament and of the Council of 7 September 2005 on the recognition of professional qualifications (OJ L 255, 30.9.2005, p. 22), the statutory professional title, the state in which it was conferred and, if it belongs to a chamber, professional association or similar body, its name,
7. any general terms and conditions it uses,
8. any contract clauses it uses on the law applicable to the contract or on the place of jurisdiction,
9. any guarantees that go beyond the statutory warranty rights,
10. the essential characteristics of the service, unless they are already apparent from the context,
11. if professional liability insurance exists, details of it, in particular the name and address of the insurer and the territorial scope of cover."""@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DLInfoV-s2-1-q1 .

lg:DE-DLInfoV-source-legalStatus-db47b870273c-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "db47b870273c408ff46a28a5f9802c51fd173e7c4e3176deca1e20692a32d9ad" ;
    lgo:text "Last amended by Art. 3 of the Act of 12 May 2026 (BGBl. I No. 138)"@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translatedProperty lgo:legalStatus ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DLInfoV-source .

lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn137-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "00d90a9a80693ed004736064a808d7d105216b2d119177df510688f1f312ac80" ;
    lgo:text "(137) If a button to refuse consent is offered next to the button to give consent, its label must be unambiguous, so that users know they are not giving consent. This can be achieved with a short and concise label. A button \"Settings or refuse\" that leads to a further layer of the banner is not sufficient here."@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn137-q1 .

lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn50-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "a801dc1c10c8ae486fa1d7b7e65e429a16f996a422a9435596c26ddee1b29616" ;
    lgo:text "(50) In order to be able to prove that end users have given an unambiguous and clearly affirmative action, they must therefore be offered at least such choices whose communicative effect is equivalent. If one choice is presented precisely and has an immediate effect (e.g. an \"Accept all\" button), while the other option is kept vague and does not allow the true contrary will to be expressed with the same effort, there is a deficit of effect and information. Such a deficit is likely to lead end users to make their decision not according to their clear will, but only according to which option ends the consent request clearly faster. If users are not offered equivalent options to give or refuse consent, the requirements for valid consent are regularly not met."@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn50-q1 .

lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn53-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "bc85f669ef7dc66dd975d046a6f815f27b071cfd981a129db7656dbf6bd07e1b" ;
    lgo:text "(53) In principle, consent banners may have several layers, giving more detailed information only on a second layer of the banner, which users reach through a button or link. However, if there is already a button on the first layer of the banner with which consent can be given for various purposes, specific information on all the individual purposes must also be given on this first layer. It would be too vague merely to give generic, general or vague information on the purposes there, such as “We use cookies to offer you a better user experience”."@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn53-q1 .

lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn58-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "18929ce73b7e6ef045fd76955b45c693250e05e8447645bc53c268a1b6121ada" ;
    lgo:text "(58) Voluntariness is also noticeably affected if refusing all accesses that require consent means measurable additional effort for end users. Such additional effort is created, for example, if refusal is possible only on a second layer of the banner, and thus only with more clicks (compared with consenting). As a rule, the additional effort does not merely consist in end users having to click once more than when consenting. Rather, they must also read and understand the further information and setting options they are confronted with on a second layer of the consent dialogue, and then choose the right one among the further options. The additional effort created generally cannot be objectively justified either (e.g. by technical obstacles), but is artificially constructed. This can be inferred not least from the fact that end users are by now offered an equally simple option to refuse on a great many websites."@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn58-q1 .

lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn78-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "daf97181d0b87194a697cd1736a34d40cac6c0fe9922fc42a1eb8c7c50a80b3e" ;
    lgo:text "(78) The criterion “strictly necessary” is defined in more detail neither in the TDDDG nor in the ePrivacy Directive. The explanatory memorandum to the TDDDG, however, assumes technical necessity, which suggests a strict understanding. This means that, even for services expressly requested by end users, the exception covers only those accesses to the terminal equipment that are technically necessary to provide precisely the requested service. For the criterion of necessity within the meaning of the provision relates exclusively to the functionality of the digital service as such. An exception from the consent requirement therefore cannot be justified by storing information on, or accessing information in, the terminal equipment being economically necessary for the business model in which the digital service is embedded."@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "claude-opus-5-5" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-DSK-OH-DD-rn78-q1 .

lg:DE-EGBGB-a246a-s1-1-1-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "b8a223c0fd4f3b50d092c80c15ddf840927bc831fac2c4510489788eca543786" ;
    lgo:text "the main characteristics of the goods or services, to the extent appropriate to the means of communication and to the goods and services"@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "devin" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-EGBGB-a246a-s1-1-1-q1 .

lg:DE-EGBGB-a246a-s1-1-10-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
    lgo:reviewStatus lgv:Draft ;
    lgo:sourceTextSha256 "6d8857ac17925218cb8f3704d621f4c3330b5582fee3d4eb3ce4841b26de0c14" ;
    lgo:text "the arrangements for payment, delivery and performance, including environmentally friendly delivery options where available, the date by which the trader undertakes to deliver the goods or perform the services and, where applicable, the trader's complaint handling policy"@en ;
    lgo:translatedBy "devin" ;
    lgo:translatedOn "2026-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    lgo:translationMethod lgv:MachineTranslation ;
    lgo:translationOf lg:DE-EGBGB-a246a-s1-1-10-q1 .

lg:DE-EGBGB-a246a-s1-1-11-q1-en
    a lgo:Translation ;
